<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–<a href="/years/1158">1158</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Saint Rögnvald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson was earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> from about 1136 to 1158, a nephew of the murdered Earl Magnus. He won the earldom with Norwegian help and in 1137 began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> in his uncle's honour. He made a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1151–1153, wrote skaldic verse, and after his murder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson.jpg" alt="Rögnvald Kali Kolsson" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Magnus_Cathedral_-_St_Rognvald_20110523.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rögnvald's father was the lendmann Kolr Kalisson and his mother Gunnhildr Erlendsdottir, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson">Magnus Erlendsson</a>, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. It was through his mother that he had a claim to the earldom. His birthplace is uncertain: some researchers place it near the medieval Fjære Church in the present-day Grimstad Municipality, others at the king's estate at Lista in Agder, where his family owned several farms.</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd I of Norway</a> appointed him Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> in 1129. Kali then took the name Rögnvald, after Earl Rögnvald Brusason, whom his mother thought the ablest of the earls of Orkney, in the hope that the name would bring him luck. He should have held half of Orkney, as Magnus had, but his second cousin Paul Haakonsson had made himself sole ruler of the islands and would not give up any of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rögnvald remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> as one of the leading men of King Harald Gille and was hailed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> in 1136. In 1137 he began the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a>. He was guardian of Harald Maddadsson, the five-year-old nephew of Paul Haakonsson, and in 1138 made him earl alongside himself. Harald had inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which brought that area under Rögnvald's control as well.</p><p>In 1151 he set out on a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, which takes up five chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a>. The saga says little of the stay in the Holy Land itself and dwells on fighting and feasting during the voyage. It credits the idea to Eindridi Ungi, a distant relative, who named prestige as a reason for the expedition. With Hallr Þórarinsson, Rögnvald composed the learned skaldic poem Háttalykill inn forni, and other verses of his record the journey, among them five weeks spent in the mud of Grimsby and a swim across the River Jordan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>While he was away, King David I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> granted half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> to Erlend Haraldsson, who was later driven out by Harald and killed in 1156. In August 1158 Rögnvald and his company of eight men were cut down by Thorbjorn Klerk, a former friend and counsellor of Harald, whom he had outlawed for a murder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a>. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a>, and miracles were reported at his grave and at the stone where he died. He is said to have been canonised by Pope Celestine III in 1192, though no surviving record confirms it. North Ronaldsay and South Ronaldsay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rögnvald Kali Kolsson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Earl Magnus, murdered around 1117, was venerated as a saint, and his nephew <b>Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</b> began St Magnus Cathedral in Kirkwall in 1137.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rogaland. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.42,-3.5&amp;year=1137" aria-label="Caithness on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4476 193 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Rögnvald Kali Kolsson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4926 463h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4926 463h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=58.42,-3.5&amp;year=1137">What was here in 1137? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
